# One deploy per task (Build-a-Wig)

**Each user prompt / completed task = exactly ONE `git commit` + ONE `git push` to `master`.**  
Every extra push to `master` triggers **another Vercel production deploy** (including **`git push --force-with-lease`** to amend a message).

## Required order (when code or docs change)

1. **Finish all code/docs edits** for this user request.
2. **Append `motherboard/MEMORY.md` first** (auto-add) — entire conversation so far. See `motherboard/ADDING.md`. **Do this before any `git commit` or `./scripts/agent-commit.sh` attempt.**
3. Run **`./scripts/agent-commit.sh "descriptive message"`** once — it stages everything, commits, pushes. **Do not** hand-run separate `git commit` / `git push` steps unless you mirror the same checks.

## Forbidden (each causes an extra deploy)

- Push code, then a follow-up commit for **`MEMORY.md`** or **`Motherboard: …`**.
- **Two pushes in one task** — e.g. push with a bad/`test` message, then **`git commit --amend`** + **`git push --force-with-lease`** to fix it.
- **`git push --force`** / **`--force-with-lease`** on `master` to repair the same task (use only if the user explicitly asks to rewrite history).
- Running **`./scripts/agent-commit.sh`** twice for the same task (first push already deployed).
- Debug commits with messages like **`test`**, **`wip`**, or **`Motherboard:`** as a second commit after feature code.
- **Splitting one founder request** into a code commit plus a follow-up docs/MEMORY/handoff/CONCLUSION-convention commit.

## Task completeness (before commit)

One founder request = one governed task. Stage **all** artifacts before the single commit:

- Application code and tests
- Generated build outputs required at runtime (if not produced solely on Vercel)
- Documentation and forensic reports
- `CURRENT_HANDOFF.md` and `KNOWN_BLOCKERS.md` when operational state changes
- `motherboard/MEMORY.md` (and minimal `CORE.md` if needed)
- Commit-discipline or agent-convention doc updates when the task changes process rules

Do **not** interpret “one commit” as “commit code first, documentation later.” The plain-text **CONCLUSION** box is normally response-only; if a task changes that convention in the repo, that doc update ships in the **same** commit.

## If something is wrong before the first push

- **`git commit --amend`** (fix message or add forgotten files) — then **one** `git push`. Still only one push.

## If you already pushed once in this task

- **Stop.** Do **not** push again to “fix” message, MEMORY, or a typo — that is a **second deploy**. Note the gap; fold fixes into the **next** user-requested change, or ask the user before a second push.

## Memory-only turns

Q&A with no code change: a MEMORY-only commit is OK. Any code/doc change → **`MEMORY.md` in that same commit**, one push.
